**Step 1: Apply quadratic reciprocity.**

For an odd prime $p \neq 11$, quadratic reciprocity gives
$$\left(\frac{11}{p}\right)\left(\frac{p}{11}\right) = (-1)^{\frac{11-1}{2}\cdot\frac{p-1}{2}} = (-1)^{5\cdot\frac{p-1}{2}}.$$
Since $5$ is odd, $5\cdot\frac{p-1}{2}$ has the same parity as $\frac{p-1}{2}$, so
$$\left(\frac{11}{p}\right) = (-1)^{\frac{p-1}{2}}\left(\frac{p}{11}\right).$$

**Step 2: Identify the two factors.**

- $(-1)^{\frac{p-1}{2}}$ depends only on $p \bmod 4$: it is $+1$ if $p\equiv 1\pmod 4$ and $-1$ if $p\equiv 3\pmod 4$.
- $\left(\frac{p}{11}\right)$ depends only on $p \bmod 11$, since $11$ is prime.

Therefore $\left(\frac{11}{p}\right)$ depends only on $p \bmod 4$ and $p \bmod 11$, hence (by CRT, since $\gcd(4,11)=1$) only on $p \bmod 44$. Taking $n = 44$ works, with the only exceptions being $p=2$ and $p=11$ (finitely many).

**Step 3: Minimality of $n=44$.**

The proper divisors of $44$ are $1,2,4,11,22$. For each we exhibit primes $p\equiv q\pmod d$ with $\left(\frac{11}{p}\right)\neq\left(\frac{11}{q}\right)$:

- $d=4$: $p=5$, $q=13$, both $\equiv 1\pmod 4$. $\left(\frac{11}{5}\right)=+1$ (since $f(5)=(-1)^2\cdot(5/11)=1\cdot 1=1$), $\left(\frac{11}{13}\right)=-1$ (since $f(13)=1\cdot(2/11)=-1$). Different.
- $d=11$: $p=23$, $q=89$, both $\equiv 1\pmod{11}$. $f(23)=(-1)^{11}\cdot 1 = -1$, $f(89)=(-1)^{44}\cdot 1=+1$. Different.
- $d=22$: $p=23$, $q=89$, both $\equiv 1\pmod{22}$. Same as above; different.
- $d=1,2$: the symbol is not constant (e.g. $f(5)=1$, $f(3)=-1$).

Hence no proper divisor of $44$ suffices, so $n=44$ is minimal.

**Step 4: Determine the classes with $\left(\frac{11}{p}\right)=1$.**

The quadratic residues modulo $11$ are $\{1,3,4,5,9\}$. For $a$ ranging over the $20$ units modulo $44$ (odd numbers in $\{1,\dots,43\}$ not divisible by $11$), compute
$$f(a) = (-1)^{\frac{a-1}{2}}\cdot\left(\frac{a}{11}\right).$$

| $a$ | $a\bmod 4$ | $a\bmod 11$ | sign | $(a/11)$ | $f(a)$ |
|-----|-----------|------------|------|---------|-------|
| 1 | 1 | 1 | $+1$ | $+1$ | $+1$ |
| 3 | 3 | 3 | $-1$ | $+1$ | $-1$ |
| 5 | 1 | 5 | $+1$ | $+1$ | $+1$ |
| 7 | 3 | 7 | $-1$ | $-1$ | $+1$ |
| 9 | 1 | 9 | $+1$ | $+1$ | $+1$ |
| 13 | 1 | 2 | $+1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |
| 15 | 3 | 4 | $-1$ | $+1$ | $-1$ |
| 17 | 1 | 6 | $+1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |
| 19 | 3 | 8 | $-1$ | $-1$ | $+1$ |
| 21 | 1 | 10 | $+1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |
| 23 | 3 | 1 | $-1$ | $+1$ | $-1$ |
| 25 | 1 | 3 | $+1$ | $+1$ | $+1$ |
| 27 | 3 | 5 | $-1$ | $+1$ | $-1$ |
| 29 | 1 | 7 | $+1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |
| 31 | 3 | 9 | $-1$ | $+1$ | $-1$ |
| 35 | 3 | 2 | $-1$ | $-1$ | $+1$ |
| 37 | 1 | 4 | $+1$ | $+1$ | $+1$ |
| 39 | 3 | 6 | $-1$ | $-1$ | $+1$ |
| 41 | 1 | 8 | $+1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |
| 43 | 3 | 10 | $-1$ | $-1$ | $+1$ |

The classes with $f(a)=+1$ are exactly
$$a \in \{1,\,5,\,7,\,9,\,19,\,25,\,35,\,37,\,39,\,43\} \pmod{44},$$
which is $10$ of the $20$ unit classes, as expected.

---

**Conclusion.** The minimal natural number is $n = 44$, and for primes $p\neq 2, 11$,
$$\left(\frac{11}{p}\right)=1 \iff p \equiv 1,\,5,\,7,\,9,\,19,\,25,\,35,\,37,\,39,\text{ or }43 \pmod{44}.$$

